The waveform is \[g(x)=
\begin{cases}
-k, &amp; -\pi&lt;x\le0,\\
+k, &amp; 0&lt;x\le\pi,\\
0, &amp; \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}\] The periodic signal is \[f(x)=\sum_{m=-\infty}^{\infty}g(x+2\pi m),\] which is an odd square wave. Hence, \[a_0=0,\qquad a_n=0.\] For the sine coefficients, \[b_n=\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(x)\sin(nx)\,dx
=\frac{2}{\pi}\int_{0}^{\pi}k\sin(nx)\,dx.\] Therefore, \[b_n=\frac{2k}{\pi}
\left[\frac{1-\cos(n\pi)}{n}\right].\] For odd \(n\), \[b_n=\frac{4k}{n\pi}.\] Hence, for the third harmonic, \[a_3+b_3=0+\frac{4k}{3\pi}.\] Given, \[\frac{4k}{3\pi}=\frac{2}{3\pi},\] which gives \[k=\frac{1}{2}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(b) }\frac{1}{2}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>An odd periodic signal has only sine terms in its trigonometric Fourier series.</li><li>For a square wave of amplitude \(k\), \(b_n=\dfrac{4k}{n\pi}\) for odd \(n\) and \(b_n=0\) for even \(n\).</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not compute cosine coefficients for an odd function.</li><li>Remember that only odd harmonics are present in this square wave.</li></ul></div>

