Given, \[\vec{v}=5\hat{x}\] \[\vec{E}=4\hat{y}\] \[\vec{B}=-6\hat{z}\] and the charge \(q\) is positive. The Lorentz force acting on the charge is \[\vec{F}=q\left(\vec{E}+\vec{v}\times\vec{B}\right)\] First, evaluate the magnetic force component. \[\vec{v}\times\vec{B}
=
(5\hat{x})\times(-6\hat{z})\] Using \[\hat{x}\times\hat{z}=-\hat{y}\] we get \[\vec{v}\times\vec{B}
=
(5)(-6)(-\hat{y})
=
30\hat{y}\] Substituting into the Lorentz force equation, \[\vec{F}
=
q\left(4\hat{y}+30\hat{y}\right)\] \[\vec{F}
=
34q\,\hat{y}\] Hence, the force acts along the positive \(y\)-direction, i.e., along the direction of the electric field and perpendicular to the magnetic field. \[\boxed{B}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Lorentz force: \[\vec{F}=q\left(\vec{E}+\vec{v}\times\vec{B}\right)\]</li><li>Unit vector identities: \[\hat{x}\times\hat{y}=\hat{z},\qquad
\hat{y}\times\hat{z}=\hat{x},\qquad
\hat{z}\times\hat{x}=\hat{y}\]</li><li>Reversing the order changes the sign of the cross product.</li><li>For a positive charge, the force direction is the same as \(\vec{E}+\vec{v}\times\vec{B}\).</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not use \(\hat{x}\times\hat{z}=\hat{y}\); the correct relation is \(\hat{x}\times\hat{z}=-\hat{y}\).</li><li>Do not forget to include both the electric and magnetic force components.</li><li>Remember that the direction reverses for a negative charge.</li></ul></div>

The loop lies in the \(XY\) plane. Hence, its area vector is along the \(Z\)-axis: \[d\vec{A}=dA\,\hat{z}\] Only the \(\hat{z}\) component of magnetic field contributes to flux. For region \(a_1\), \[\vec{B}_1
=
\frac{m}{\sqrt{2}}\sin(\omega t)(\hat{x}+\hat{z})\] \[\phi_1
=
\int_{a_1}\vec{B}_1\cdot d\vec{A}
=
\frac{m a_1}{\sqrt{2}}\sin(\omega t)\] For region \(a_2\), \[\vec{B}_2
=
-\frac{n}{\sqrt{2}}
\cos\left(2\omega t+\frac{\pi}{4}\right)
(\hat{y}+\hat{z})\] \[\phi_2
=
\int_{a_2}\vec{B}_2\cdot d\vec{A}
=
-\frac{n a_2}{\sqrt{2}}
\cos\left(2\omega t+\frac{\pi}{4}\right)\] Total flux is \[\phi
=
\frac{m a_1}{\sqrt{2}}\sin(\omega t)
-
\frac{n a_2}{\sqrt{2}}
\cos\left(2\omega t+\frac{\pi}{4}\right)\] By Faraday's law, \[e(t)=-\frac{d\phi}{dt}\] \[e(t)
=
-\frac{m\omega a_1}{\sqrt{2}}\cos(\omega t)
-
\sqrt{2}n\omega a_2
\sin\left(2\omega t+\frac{\pi}{4}\right)\] The RMS values of the two different-frequency components are combined by root-sum-square: \[E_{\text{rms}}
=
\sqrt{
\left(\frac{m\omega a_1}{2}\right)^2
+
(n\omega a_2)^2
}\] \[E_{\text{rms}}
=
\sqrt{
\frac{a_1^2\omega^2m^2+4a_2^2\omega^2n^2}{4}
}\] Therefore, the correct option is \[\boxed{A}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For a loop in the \(XY\) plane, only the \(\hat{z}\) component of \(\vec{B}\) contributes to flux.</li><li>Induced emf is \(e(t)=-\dfrac{d\phi}{dt}\).</li><li>RMS values of different-frequency sinusoidal components are combined using root-sum-square.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not use the full magnitude of \(\vec{B}\); only the normal component links flux.</li><li>Do not miss the factor \(2\omega\) while differentiating \(\cos\left(2\omega t+\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right)\).</li><li>Do not directly add RMS values of different-frequency components.</li></ul></div>

The fundamental relationship for a transformer is derived from the EMF equation: \[V \approx 4.44 f N \phi_{m}\] Since \[\phi_{m} = B_{m}A\] we get: \[B_{m} \propto \frac{V}{f}\] Comparing the operating conditions: \[\begin{aligned}
\text{India:} \quad 
V_{1} &amp;= 220\,\text{V}, \qquad f_{1} = 50\,\text{Hz} \\
\frac{V_{1}}{f_{1}} &amp;= \frac{220}{50} = 4.4
\end{aligned}\] \[\begin{aligned}
\text{USA:} \quad 
V_{2} &amp;= 110\,\text{V}, \qquad f_{2} = 60\,\text{Hz} \\
\frac{V_{2}}{f_{2}} &amp;= \frac{110}{60} \approx 1.83
\end{aligned}\] <ul><li><strong>A. No-load current drawn would be smaller in the USA: Correct</strong><br/>Since the \(\dfrac{V}{f}\) ratio (and hence \(B_{m}\)) is significantly lower in the USA, the core operates much farther from saturation. Therefore, the magnetizing current required to establish the flux is much smaller.</li><li><strong>B. For the same load current, the losses would be higher in the USA: Incorrect</strong><br/>Copper losses: \[P_{cu} = I^{2}R\] remain unchanged for the same current. Core losses generally decrease because the reduction in flux density dominates the effect of increased frequency.</li><li><strong>C. Peak magnetic flux density (\(B_{m}\)) would be higher in the USA: Incorrect</strong><br/>Since: \[\left(\frac{V}{f}\right)_{\text{USA}}
    &lt;
    \left(\frac{V}{f}\right)_{\text{India}}\] the peak flux density in the USA is lower.</li><li><strong>D. Eddy current losses would be approximately \(44\%\) higher in the USA: Incorrect</strong><br/>Eddy current loss: \[P_{e} \propto f^{2}B_{m}^{2}\] Since: \[B_{m} \propto \frac{V}{f}\] we obtain: \[P_{e} \propto V^{2}\] Therefore: \[\frac{P_{e(\text{USA})}}{P_{e(\text{India})}}
    =
    \left(\frac{110}{220}\right)^{2}
    =
    \frac{1}{4}\] Hence, eddy current losses reduce to nearly \(25\%\) of the original value.</li></ul> Therefore, the only correct statement is: \[\boxed{\text{A}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Peak flux density in a transformer core is proportional to the \(\dfrac{V}{f}\) ratio.</li><li>Magnetizing current decreases significantly when the transformer operates at lower flux density.</li><li>Eddy current loss: \[P_{e} \propto f^{2}B_{m}^{2}\]</li><li>Hysteresis loss: \[P_{h} \propto fB_{m}^{x}\] where \(x\) is the Steinmetz exponent.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not assume that higher frequency always increases total core losses.</li><li>Avoid comparing only voltage values; transformer flux depends on the \(\dfrac{V}{f}\) ratio.</li><li>Do not confuse magnetic flux \(\phi_{m}\) with flux density \(B_{m}\).</li></ul></div>

Given a two-winding transformer with: <ul><li>Power rating \(S_{2W} = 15\,\text{kVA}\)</li><li>Voltage ratings \(V_{HV} = 1100\,\text{V}\), \(V_{LV} = 220\,\text{V}\)</li><li>Reconfigured autotransformer ratio: \(1.32\,\text{kV} / 1.1\,\text{kV}\)</li></ul> <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 1</div>The autotransformer rating \(S_{AT}\) is related to the two-winding rating \(S_{2W}\) by the formula: \[S_{AT} = S_{2W} \times \left( \frac{V_{High,AT}}{V_{series}} \right)\] The series winding voltage (\(V_{series}\)) is the difference between the high and low voltages of the autotransformer: \[V_{series} = 1.32\,\text{kV} - 1.1\,\text{kV}
= 0.22\,\text{kV}
= 220\,\text{V}\] Substituting the values: \[S_{AT}
= 15\,\text{kVA}
\times
\left(
\frac{1.32\,\text{kV}}{0.22\,\text{kV}}
\right)
= 15 \times 6
= 90\,\text{kVA}\]</div> <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 2</div><span class="missing-fig">[figure unavailable]</span> The rated currents for the two-winding transformer are: \[I_{HV} = \frac{15 \times 10^{3}}{1100}
= 13.636\,\text{A}\] \[I_{LV} = \frac{15 \times 10^{3}}{220}
= 68.182\,\text{A}\] In the \(1.32\,\text{kV} / 1.1\,\text{kV}\) configuration, the \(220\,\text{V}\) winding is the series winding, and the \(1100\,\text{V}\) winding is the common winding. The current \(I_{1}\) (input current at the \(1.32\,\text{kV}\) side) must be limited by the series winding current. <span class="missing-fig">[figure unavailable]</span> \[S_{auto}
= V_{1} \times I_{series}
= 1320\,\text{V} \times 68.182\,\text{A}
= 90000\,\text{VA}
= 90\,\text{kVA}\]</div> \[\boxed{D}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>The kVA rating of an autotransformer is always higher than its two-winding counterpart because power is transferred both conductively and inductively.</li><li>\(S_{AT} = \frac{1}{1-k} S_{2W}\), where \(k\) is the transformation ratio (\(V_{low}/V_{high}\)).</li><li>The series winding must be able to carry the full rated current of the winding used for that section.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not confuse \(V_{series}\) with the low-voltage rating of the autotransformer; \(V_{series}\) is specifically the voltage across the winding not common to both sides.</li><li>Ensure units are consistent (converting kV to V) before performing division or multiplication.</li></ul></div>

In a double-squirrel-cage induction motor, the outer cage has high resistance and low reactance, whereas the inner cage has low resistance and high reactance. At starting, the rotor frequency is equal to the supply frequency, so the rotor reactance is high. The current therefore prefers the path having lower impedance, i.e., the outer cage. \[Z_{\text{outer}}=\sqrt{R_{\text{outer}}^{2}+X_{\text{outer}}^{2}
}\] \[Z_{\text{inner}}=\sqrt{R_{\text{inner}}^{2}+X_{\text{inner}}^{2}
}\] Since \[X_{\text{inner}} \gg X_{\text{outer}}\] at starting, \[Z_{\text{inner}} &gt; Z_{\text{outer}}\] Hence, a larger portion of the rotor current flows in the outer cage. At normal operating speed, the rotor frequency becomes very small, making reactances negligible. Due to its lower resistance, the inner cage carries most of the current. \[\boxed{\text{A}}\]

